  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-intro">
      <span class="eyebrow">The wrong comparison</span>
      <h2>A lower management fee can still leave owner net exposed.</h2>
      <p>The headline <a href="/property-management/vacation-rental-management-fees-florida/">management fee</a> matters, but it is not the whole economic picture. If most bookings arrive through high-drag channels, if direct mix is weak, if cleaning-heavy subtotals inflate OTA fees, or if rate decisions are not explained clearly, you can lose margin even when the management fee looks competitive.</p>
      <p><strong>The question is not only "What do they charge?" It is "What actually reaches my owner statement?"</strong></p>
    </div>
    <div class="stack-grid">
      <article class="stack-card">
        <span class="stack-metric">Headline fee</span>
        <h3>Management fee</h3>
        <p>Your fee still matters, but it is only one piece of the owner-net equation. A lower-looking manager can still underperform if the calendar, channel mix, or operations are weak.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="stack-card">
        <span class="stack-metric">Observed Seascape portfolio data</span>
        <h3>OTA host drag</h3>
        <p>Airbnb's fee is not just a platform line item. Airbnb host-fee drag averaged 13.4% on Seascape bookings after October 2025, including fees applied to the full subtotal.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="stack-card">
        <span class="stack-metric">Observed Seascape portfolio data</span>
        <h3>Direct payment cost</h3>
        <p>Direct processing at about 2.9% does not solve everything by itself. It does show how much owner margin can move when a booking is acquired without the heavier OTA layer.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="stack-card">
        <span class="stack-metric">Property-level review</span>
        <h3>Rate and operations discipline</h3>
        <p>Discount-heavy fills, weak premium-week protection, turnover misses, and poor owner reporting rarely show up cleanly in a flat management-fee comparison. They still hit the same payout.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-intro">
      <span class="eyebrow">Channel drag</span>
      <h2>The channel can move owner net as much as the fee conversation.</h2>
      <p>Airbnb's host-fee drag averaged 13.4% on Seascape bookings after October 2025. Direct payment cost is 2.9%. That gap is why <a href="/property-management/maximize-vacation-rental-income-florida/">direct mix</a> belongs in the owner-net conversation.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-grid">
      <div class="compare-panel">
        <span class="eyebrow">Observed Seascape portfolio data</span>
        <h3>Airbnb Drag vs Direct Payment Cost</h3>
        <div class="bar-list">
          <div class="bar-row">
            <div class="bar-label">Airbnb host drag</div>
            <div class="bar-track"><span class="bar-fill" style="width:89%;background:var(--gold)"></span></div>
            <div class="bar-value">13.4%</div>
          </div>
          <div class="bar-row">
            <div class="bar-label">Direct payment cost</div>
            <div class="bar-track"><span class="bar-fill" style="width:19%;background:var(--brand-dark)"></span></div>
            <div class="bar-value">2.9%</div>
          </div>
          <div class="bar-row">
            <div class="bar-label">Gap to audit</div>
            <div class="bar-track"><span class="bar-fill" style="width:70%;background:rgba(95,138,139,.38)"></span></div>
            <div class="bar-value">10.5 pts</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="mini-note">Observed Seascape channel-cost references. Airbnb drag is an observed Seascape average; direct cost is a processing reference. This chart is not a market-wide fee survey.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-panel">
        <span class="eyebrow">Where Owner Revenue Actually Leaks</span>
        <ul class="quick-list">
          <li>Airbnb takes its cut on the full subtotal, including cleaning-heavy stays.</li>
          <li>A booking can look fine on occupancy while still leaking margin through the channel mix.</li>
          <li>Your question is not whether direct fixes everything. It is whether the current mix is more expensive than it needs to be.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="benchmark-math">
    <div class="section-intro">
      <span class="eyebrow">Sample leak math</span>
      <h2>A $10,000 booking can leak about $1,050 before the management fee even enters the conversation.</h2>
      <p>Here is the simplest version of the benchmark math. It uses Seascape's observed 13.4% Airbnb host-fee drag and the 2.9% direct payment cost reference on the same booking subtotal.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="math-example">
      <p>Use this as worked scenario math, not as a quote or forecast. The point is to show how much owner margin can move when the booking stays in a high-drag channel instead of a lower-drag direct-payment path.</p>
      <div class="math-card-grid">
        <article class="math-card">
          <span class="stack-metric">Scenario math, not a forecast</span>
