        <article class="math-card">
          <span class="stack-metric">Scenario math using current direct cost reference</span>
          <span class="math-value">$290</span>
          <h3>Direct payment cost</h3>
          <p>2.9% applied to the same $10,000 subtotal.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="math-card">
          <span class="stack-metric">Scenario math, not a forecast</span>
          <span class="math-value">$1,050</span>
          <h3>Gap before management fee</h3>
          <p>That is the margin difference still in play before the management-fee quote even gets compared.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="math-read-grid">
        <article class="math-read-card">
          <h3>Same fee, different owner net</h3>
          <p>If two managers charge the same percentage but one leaves more bookings inside high-drag channels, the owner statement still lands differently.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="math-read-card">
          <h3>Direct mix changes the math fast</h3>
          <p>One qualified direct booking can move more owner margin than small headline-fee differences that look dramatic in a sales pitch.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="math-read-card">
          <h3>Clear reporting makes the leak visible</h3>
          <p>Owners need to see what is proven, what is likely leaking, and what is still unknown before they decide whether to switch.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="mini-note">Scenario math showing how channel mix and fee drag can change owner net. It is not a forecast, guarantee, or property-specific quote.</p>
  </section>

